# Existing rules, kept.
codex-rs/app-server-protocol/schema/** linguist-generated
codex-rs/hooks/schema/generated/** linguist-generated

# ============================================================================
# Line endings. THE PROMPT IS A MEASURED SURFACE, SO IT CANNOT DEPEND ON THE
# MACHINE THAT BUILT IT.
#
# `include_str!` copies a file from the working tree into the binary verbatim.
# With `core.autocrlf=true` - the Windows default, and what this fork's main
# checkout uses - every one of those files arrives CRLF while its blob is LF.
# Measured on 2026-09-22: of the 95 text assets embedded this way, 93 differed
# from the repository, 10,264 bytes in total. Among them are `models.json`
# (1,464 lines), `prompt.md` (275), `apply_patch_prose.md`, `apply_patch.lark`
# and all of `prompts/templates/**` - that is, most of what the model reads.
#
# JSON string literals are immune, because their newlines are `\n` escapes;
# Markdown, Lark and plain text are not. So the damage is invisible in a diff
# and shows up only as two builds of one commit sending different bytes.
#
# `eol=lf` fixes it at the source: the checkout is LF on every platform,
# regardless of anyone's `core.autocrlf`. It applies at checkout time, so a
# clone that predates this line keeps its CRLF until the tree is renormalised:
#
#     git add --renormalize .        (then re-checkout, see scripts/merge/install)
#
# `text=auto` leaves binaries alone - git detects them - and the explicit
# `binary` rules below say so anyway for the ones worth being sure about.
# ============================================================================
*         text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch is the one format that genuinely wants CRLF: `cmd.exe` can
# mis-parse a label or a line continuation without it.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf

# ============================================================================
# Merge behaviour for this fork.
#
# Two kinds of file made almost every conflict in the 2026-09-20 merge of 1,050
# upstream commits, and neither was a disagreement about behaviour:
#
#   the rename   our product-name change meets upstream's edit on the same line.
#                Roughly nine of every ten conflicts. Handled by `rename` below.
#   generated    snapshots and schemas are outputs. Resolving one by hand is
#                wasted work: regenerating overwrites whatever was decided.
#
# Drivers must be registered once per clone. Git will not run a driver it has
# not been told about; it falls back to the ordinary merge without saying so:
#
#     pwsh scripts/merge/install.ps1        (or: sh scripts/merge/install.sh)
#
# To check a driver still does what it claims, replayed against the 2026-09-20
# merge's real conflicts:
#
#     python scripts/merge/test-driver.py
#
# Git applies the LAST matching line, so the broad rules come first here and the
# generated-output overrides come last.
#
# THIS FILE MUST NEVER CONFLICT. A `.gitattributes` with conflict markers in it is
# not a valid attributes file, so git stops applying every rule in it - silently,
# with only `... is not a valid attribute name` on stderr - and the merge falls
# back to the ordinary one. Found the hard way: a trial merge with the drivers
# supposedly enabled produced 611 conflicts instead of 256, because this file had
# conflicted first and disabled them. Hence the `merge=ours` on the line below.
# ============================================================================
.gitattributes  merge=ours

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the rename
# Speaks our side back in upstream's words, merges, then restores only the names
# the running program reads (scripts/merge/rename-map.txt): SUFFICE_HOME, the
# .suffice directories, the renamed enum variants and the SDK's class.
#
# The product name is left as upstream writes it. That is deliberate - it costs
# nothing, and it is what the conflicts were made of. A file that passes through
# this driver therefore stops carrying the cosmetic rename, and the tree
# converges on upstream's wording while keeping this fork's identity on disk.
#
# A real disagreement - both sides changing the same logic - still conflicts,
# with markers. This removes noise, not decisions.
#
# The list below is not a guess. Of the 879 files both sides changed in the
# 2026-09-20 merge, 592 carried the product name; every extension among them is
# covered here or by `generated` below, and the second group exists because it
# was not: `.html`, `.bazel` and `.ipynb` each had a file that carried the name
# and had to be resolved by hand.
*.rs      merge=rename
*.py      merge=rename
*.ts      merge=rename
*.tsx     merge=rename
*.js      merge=rename
*.md      merge=rename
*.toml    merge=rename
*.json    merge=rename
*.sh      merge=rename
*.ps1     merge=rename
*.txt     merge=rename
*.lark    merge=rename

# Missed by the list above in the 2026-09-20 merge, plus their near neighbours.
*.html    merge=rename
*.css     merge=rename
*.xml     merge=rename
*.yml     merge=rename
*.yaml    merge=rename
*.ipynb   merge=rename
*.sbpl    merge=rename
*.bazel   merge=rename
*.bzl     merge=rename
BUILD     merge=rename
*.mjs     merge=rename
*.cjs     merge=rename
*.jsx     merge=rename
*.mts     merge=rename
*.cts     merge=rename
*.proto   merge=rename

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- generated
# Keeps our copy and reports no conflict. The copy is stale either way until it
# is regenerated, so the merge should not stop to ask which stale copy is
# preferred. This is what absorbed 103 of the 108 name-carrying conflicts the
# rename list does not reach - almost all of them `.snap`.
#
# REGENERATING IS NOT OPTIONAL. After a merge that touches these, run the
# generators. Until then the snapshot tests fail, which is the reminder:
#
#     cargo insta accept
#     cargo run -p codex-config-schema
*.snap                                  merge=generated
codex-rs/core/config.schema.json        merge=generated
codex-rs/app-server-protocol/schema/**  linguist-generated merge=generated
codex-rs/hooks/schema/generated/**      linguist-generated merge=generated
codex-rs/protocol/schema/**             merge=generated

# `.zst` is binary; without an explicit driver git refuses to merge it and
# leaves the conflict for a person who cannot read it either.
*.zst                                   binary merge=generated

# A lock file is an output of the manifests, which have already been merged by
# the time this is consulted, so keeping ours and rebuilding is the answer a
# person would reach anyway - and a three-way merge of one is famously bad.
#
# REGENERATE, and expect it to be wrong until you do: if upstream added a
# dependency, `cargo build --offline` fails on the kept lock file until a
# `cargo fetch` with network resolves it. That failure is the reminder.
Cargo.lock                              merge=generated
